oops.  I thought that's what {du'u} was.  I thought {se du'u} was "the
sentence that expresses a bridi".  So I would think that {se du'u} is the
actual sentence and {du'u} is the basic relationship that the {se du'u} is
expressing.

On Fri, Oct 1, 2010 at 11:52 AM, Kevin Reid <kpreid@switchb.org> wrote:

> On Oct 1, 2010, at 11:24, Alex Rozenshteyn wrote:
>
>  Is there a way to say something like "So you say that I am fat?"
>>
>> The problem is that cusku takes a (sedu'u/text/lu'e concept), and not a
>> more general abstraction.
>>
>
> "se du'u" is what you want, not "a more general abstraction".
>
> Without considering the English "So ...?" construct, this is:
>
>  do   cusku  lo se du'u  mi    plana
>
>  You  say    that        I  am fat
>
> "lo se du'u" is, in general, what you use where you could use a quotation
> but are instead expressing the concept in your own words. "You" said
> something whose meaning was "I am fat", but you're not saying what words
> were said.
>
> --
